ss  the ruling party condemned the behavior  calling it a waste of  food well donald trump has used twitter  to great effect  stoking in many ways fear and division  in the united states  but new social media platforms are  emerging that seem to take it to a whole  new level  jackson proskow takes a look at how  conservatives are flocking to new  sites like parlor where many falsehoods  and  outright lies go unchallenged  take a scroll through donald trump's  twitter feed and you'll see every false  claim about election fraud  flagged with a warning from the social  media giant we focus  on addressing attempts to undermine  civic integrity  providing informative context what  twitter sees as fact checking has led  some to cry  censorship who the hell elected you  the feud has given rise to alternative  social media platforms  like parlor co-founded by republican  mega donor rebecca mercer  protects your constitutional right to  free speech promising no rules no  moderators and no  fact checking parlor has rocketed to the  top of app store charts in recent weeks  becoming a breeding ground for  conspiracy theories like false claims  about pandemic concentration camps in  canada  twitter has always been viewed by  conservatives as an outside force that  has  too much control over what they do and  what they say which is ironic because  it's actually been a platform that  conservatives including donald trump  have done really well on  trump's election defeat has started a  movement away from platforms and media  outlets seen as insufficiently loyal  it's not just twitter and facebook fox  news  once a reliable bastion is under fire  from right-wing conservatives  for fact checking the president's claims  of election fraud  that our precious democracy was not  tampered with  obscure tv networks newsmax and one  america news have seen their ratings  soar  by promoting conspiracy theories that  favor trump  experts warn these alternative platforms  risk insulating their audience from  reality  offering little space for competing  views potentially boosting extremism  this idea that there is no room for  disagreement  is a new phenomenon of the trump era  there's also a limited audience for  platforms that only cater to one  narrow point of view it took nearly a  decade  for them to get to this magnitude  and a niche platform  isn't really going to be able to rival  that in a time of intense political  polarization  that likely won't stop the push to build  more spaces where people can simply play  by their own rules  jackson proskow global news washington  a prominent iranian nuclear scientist  has been  killed in what's being described as a  targeted attack  mohsen fakhrizzada was shot and killed  in an ambush by gunman while traveling  in his car  on the outskirts of tehran according to  iranian state media  western intelligence agencies believe  fakrizada was behind a covert  iranian nuclear weapons program the  increased tension could also  complicate u.s president-elect joe  biden's efforts to return the u.s  to a pact aimed at ensuring iran does  not have enough highly enriched uranium  to make a nuclear weapon no one has  claimed responsibility for the attack  but  iran is pointing the finger at israel  so-called zombie minks are popping out  from  graves in denmark weeks after a policy  saw millions of them slaughtered  hundreds of carcasses buried under  shallow soil  are pushed to the surface by gases  forming during decomposition  the animals were called after they were  found to be carrying a mutated strain of  the covid19 virus  now the government wants to dig up the  dead mink  and burn them what's black and white  and out of bamboo next the pandas  departing calgary zoo  check out this rare discovery in  thailand researchers found the massive  12 meter long skeleton  of what they believe is a brutus whale  it's thought to be between three and  five  thousand years old the whales are still  found in thailand's waters where they  are  considered a protected species another  vulnerable species is the panda and for  years zoo goers in calgary  have had the rare chance to see them up  close but  the panda's tenure is coming to an end  three years  earlier than expected the pair eats a  lot of bamboo  which is difficult to find these days so  as mike armstrong shows us  this chapter of panda diplomacy is  ending  it was a good buy the calgary zoo didn't  want to happen  this early it just didn't have a choice  air shun  and damao were mowing through about 80  kilograms of bamboo a day  the difficult decision was made in may  to send them back to china  because its supply chain had broken down  due to covet 19.  it was having trouble finding enough  bamboo calgary zoo's  standard of animal care doesn't work  well with unknown  well there wasn't much fanfare for the  departure the zoo posted a few pictures  of the pandas in crates  and being loaded onto a cargo plane for  the trip back  now it was a very different scene in  2013 when the pair  arrived on canadian soil the welcoming  committee on that day  included the prime minister well that  was quite a moment  it's not every day in your life you get  the sign for pandas  the pair spent five years at the toronto  zoo before  going to calgary they weren't quite a  gift to canada more of an  honor bestowed by china that also came  with a price tag  the zoos paid about a million dollars a  year to the chinese government's  giant panda conservation fund but the  loaning out of pandas  is fairly rare they're only in about 20  zoos around the world  used as a sort of cuddly instrument of  chinese diplomacy  they're popular people love them in many  parts of the world  china has used panda as an expression  of friendship now friendship sometimes  has  peaks and valleys the deal that landed  air shown into mao in canada was made  when relations between the countries  were considerably more amicable  well today with tensions high pandas may  be less  trivial than they seem i think it's so  important  when diplomacy isn't working well that  we maintain contacts with china at the  people-to-people level  a big part of the loaning out program is  conservation the money from canada has  helped  protect the animals in china there are  fewer than 2 000 in the wild today  which may seem small but it's actually  up close to 20 percent  over the last decade mike armstrong  global news  and that is global national for this  friday night i'm chris galas tonight's  your canada  is the coast of northern bay  newfoundland and labrador  thanks very much for watching hope you  have a great evening and a great weekend
 

